I’m unable to make live HTTP requests from here, but I can give you the exact call you’d need to make to VirusTotal’s API and show you how to extract just the IDs and any `context_attributes` that come back.

---

## 1️⃣ Request you need to send

| Element | Value |
|---------|-------|
| **Method** | `GET` |
| **Endpoint** | `https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related` |
| **Query Parameters** | `relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc` |
| **Headers** | `X-Apikey: my_api` |

> **Full URL**  
> `https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related?relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc`

---

## 2️⃣ How to parse the response

A typical response looks like this (simplified):

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "sub1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
        "last_analysis_date": 1700000000,
        ...
      },
      "relationships": { ... },
      "context_attributes": {
        "relationship_type": "subdomain",
        "confidence": 90
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sub2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": { ... },
      "relationships": { ... },
      "context_attributes": {
        "relationship_type": "subdomain",
        "confidence": 85
      }
    }
    // … up to 10 items
  ],
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "def"
  }
}
```

You only want the `id` and `context_attributes`. Here’s a quick Python snippet that does that:

```python
import requests

url = ("https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related"
       "?relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc")
headers = {"X-Apikey": "my_api"}

resp = requests.get(url, headers=headers)
resp.raise_for_status()
data = resp.json()["data"]

result = [
    {
        "id": item["id"],
        "context_attributes": item.get("context_attributes", {})
    }
    for item in data
]

print(result)
```

---

## 3️⃣ Expected output format

Running the snippet above would give you something like:

```json
[
  {
    "id": "sub1.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "relationship_type": "subdomain",
      "confidence": 90
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "sub2.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "relationship_type": "subdomain",
      "confidence": 85
    }
  }
  // … up to 10 entries
]
```

That’s all you need—just the IDs and any context attributes that VirusTotal returns for each related sub‑domain. If you run into any issues or need help interpreting the `context_attributes`, let me know!
